# Former Gods, Part 7
Tamil title: மாஜி கடவுள்கள் - பகுதி 7

Even today we use an ancient creation story: a vine rises from the navel of Vishnu reclining in the Milk Ocean; a lotus blooms at its tip; four-faced Brahma sits upon it; Saraswati sits upon his tongue and plays the veena; Narada sings, Nandi drums, three-eyed Siva dances, the universe rejoices, Suddhananda Bharati explains its subtle meaning, and Vasantha Kokilam turns it into sweet song.

Our intellectuals neither recognize that such stories sabotage good sense nor cease the futile work of finding inner meanings in them. They additionally make people believe our ancestors fashioned an imagination impossible elsewhere. To expose that absurdity we described the Teutonic creation myth. In imagination it was not inferior to ours. Teutons were no less devoted to belief, ritual, temples, six daily rites, priestly authority, torture of doubters, and murder of opponents. As antiquarians here speak as though accompanying Narada’s music, Teutons spoke as though they had drunk the milk flowing from Audhumla.

But no one there believes that story now, while here those refusing comparable tales are still atheists. People who once believed Ymir’s skull was the sky now fly through it and mock the Surasamharam festival we conduct from ancestral mythology. There, sky, earth, light, and sound are studied and useful knowledge spread. We preserve everything from the burning of Tripura to the glory of sacred basil through song, spectacle, and painting.

The difference is not that only our ancestors possessed wonderful imagination. Everywhere people imagined many-headed and flying gods, deities demanding women as sacrifices, and gigantic supernatural beings. Where knowledge spread, people abandoned Audhumla and Ymir and sought truth. We still call old tales useful, meaningful, holy, praiseworthy, worshipful, and worthy of preservation. The difference between us and the rest of the world is like that between our bullock cart and an aeroplane, or between spirit worship and wireless telegraphy.

Teutons discarded their meaningless account of earth’s origin. We still call tales of Mother Earth, Adisesha supporting the cosmos, and Hiranyaksha rolling up and hiding the world sacred, and conduct worship and festivals around them. Had Westerners stopped at Teutonic mythology, could they have mined the earth and supplied benefits we enjoy? Had they ended inquiry with the claim that a giant’s skull was heaven, could later research have occurred?

We enjoy its results yet show neither gratitude nor thought. Like eating fruit and spitting the rind at the gardener, we use researchers’ achievements while mocking them as spiritually ignorant and incapable of beautiful imagination. We ride a train we neither discovered nor imagined possible in order to seek a child at Rameswaram, offer hair at Tirupati, or seek Periyapalayathal’s grace. We use new things to pursue old practices. Like an employer obtaining rosewater from labourers’ sweat and mocking them, antiquarians exploit new conveniences while disparaging new knowledge.

Would they consent to live under the conditions portrayed by the fantasies they defend? No. A dim electric lamp enrages them; interruption of scientific conveniences darkens their lives. Because these facilities arrive freely without distinguishing friend from foe, they use them while asking what benefit science gives.

Their condition would be amusing if scientists imposed conditions like religious authorities. Suppose a railway examiner asked whether the world was flat or round. A mythology-trained passenger might say “flat.” The examiner could refuse a ticket: “This train runs on a spherical world discovered by sinners who abandoned ancestral belief. Travel by bullock cart upon your ancient flat world; do not seek aid from this modern demon.”

Scientific benefits are available without such conditions, like medicine without dietary restriction or peeled fruit. Because people did not labour to discover them, they do not know their worth and praise antiquity even while enjoying them. Thus trains carry pilgrims to Rameswaram, rotary presses print Ramana’s books, Sankaracharya speaks by radio, cameras photograph Garuda service, and telephones supply the time of float festivals. Is this condition right?

Using toothpaste to wash vessels, a stone-breaking tool on fruit, or a tiger rifle against a rat would be absurd. Likewise, what shall we say of using instruments of new knowledge to sustain old life? One cause is the false claim that ancient imaginative stories prove unparalleled ancestral genius and were never made elsewhere.

Friends, such tales circulated across the ancient world. Other peoples recognized their uselessness and gained new knowledge. We described the Teutonic creation myth to demonstrate this. They forgot the Teutonic story, entered Newton’s age, and passed beyond it. They moved from delighting in the cow that fed Ymir to producing calves without bulls; from calling a skull the sky to preparing to visit the moon; from calling a giant’s blood the sea to living for days beneath it in submarines.

We remain occupied publishing rebuttals concerning a forty-eighth commentary on the hymn of the fox becoming a horse, and denouncing as atheists those who ask why only we retain unbelievable stories. Is that right, proper, or fitting? Do not answer; think.

21-?/2-49

This solitary supreme god sat on a golden throne, wearing a blue turban and cloud-coloured silk. He was tall and powerfully built, but possessed only one eye. Two cosmic ravens perched on his shoulders and two wolves lay at his feet.

## Odin

One-eyed Odin was a primal deity who received worship for ages and exercised sovereignty in this world and the next. His exploits became sacred mythology and his fame hymns, celebrated by commoner, poet, king, and mendicant alike. Germany, neighbouring lands, and Britain worshipped him as God of gods. As Siva has Kailasa and Vishnu Vaikuntha, ancient Teutons assigned Odin Asgard.

Though a few now call Kailasa and Vaikuntha religious fantasy, most people still picture Siva and Sakti dancing there, Murugan’s peacock displaying its tail before Valli, six-faced Singaravelan enjoying the sight, and mouse-mounted Vinayaka thinking his brother knows such pleasures but not the taste of appam, flattened rice, and puffed grain. Denial provokes anger. Falsehoods formed while minds were confused continue through literature, music, and drama. Some know they are instruments of deceivers but remain silent from fear of the gullible.

That condition existed among Teutons before recorded history, when Odin ruled unrivalled in Asgard amid goddesses, brothers, sons, and lesser gods. All had to obey; dissenters were cast into the abyss and destroyed. Sky and earth followed his will.

From a lofty seat, one-eyed Odin supposedly saw everything. He wore blue headgear and cloud-coloured silk. Two ravens sat upon his shoulders and two wolves at his feet, like Garuda for Vishnu and the bull for Siva. The ravens travelled everywhere and reported events, giving Odin apparent omniscience. One was Thought and the other Memory; the wolves were Greed and Gluttony.

Ask today in Berlin or London for Odin’s temple and people laugh, if they respond at all. Odin is a former god, killed by clarity and vanished before reason. Yet poets once sang, rulers poured offerings, priests worshipped, and ordinary people bowed in fear while Odin sat omnipotent in temples:

“Praise to him with two cosmic ravens!
Praise, lord of immortals and mightiest god!
Praise to him with the blue turban!
One-eyed Odin, praise!”

Our mythologists know a three-eyed god; Teutonic mythologists gave theirs one eye. Odin desired divine drink in order to become radiant and divine. Its makers tested his resolve and sacrifice: would he pluck out one eye and cast it into their well? He immediately did so, drank, and became God. A god becomes a god. Today it seems unbelievable; then it was scripture and belief in it was theism.

Because it concerns another country we readily condemn such barbarity. It arose thousands of years ago before general knowledge. Yet the same confusion remains here. Those trying to remove it receive the honour of being called atheists.

We laugh at a god seeking divine drink, while our people still listen tearfully to Kurma-avatar mythology: God became a tortoise beneath Mount Meru while gods churned the Milk Ocean with Vasuki to obtain nectar. Whoever denies it is reviled as a great sinner. Long after lands that worshipped one-eyed Odin forgot him and mocked humanity’s old attempts to understand God, stories of Bhasmasura and Mohini still circulate here as religious discourse, theatre, and cinema. Nectar, Halahala poison, Mohini, and Nilakantha remain memorized, and even educated people fear calling them fantasy.

The Teutons long ago poured that stale brew into the drain and drank the waterfall of knowledge. We remain accustomed to consuming panchagavya.

Myth said Odin married many goddesses and fathered many sons. His first wife Jord resembled our Mother Earth, and their son Thor, like Murugan, was stronger than his father. Beautiful Balder was born to Frigg. Odin married nine divine maidens and had another son, Heimdall, along with a brother Loki and many other brothers: a huge family.

One may call divine spouses and children farce in a college, but can one say so among the people? Siva too has Parvati and Ganga, Murugan and Vinayaka, Vishnu as relative, Nandi and Narada for music, and a bull as vehicle. Saivites still rage at mockery. Teutons once had that rage. They believed Odin alone kept the world from destruction by ocean, built temples, and held magnificent festivals.

As our people sing hymns to Siva, child-poems to Murugan, and an agaval to Vinayaka, Teutons worshipped each member of the divine family for benefits. They recognized and discarded this ignorance; our ordinary people still consume the old fare while educated people supply its pickle.

Some argue imaginative tales may explain divinity and burn evil from minds. Yet mythology labels as divine sports acts that cannot serve even this purpose and harm character, then gives gods sacred epithets from them. Odin had forty-nine names for worship and rode the wind-swift horse Sleipnir to inspect earth and other worlds.

The same mythologists who extolled his powers also told of hardship, enemies, defeat, and flight. They did not ask whether portraying omnipotent God like a petty king was contradictory. Priests had forbidden doubt or inquiry, and mythologists confidently produced contradictions that devotion made people swallow.

Once a great war broke out between Asgard’s gods and celestial beings. Odin and his retinue were defeated; even Thor’s powerful hammer broke. Asgard was besieged. The celestial warrior Njord scattered the gods. Odin fled on Sleipnir and Thor escaped in his goat-drawn chariot. The victors installed Ull upon Odin’s throne as the new god. Odin lost divine status and fled with his family to safety, like a deposed ruler running merely to save his head.

Only Loki, Odin’s brother, pretended to worship the new god and remained in Asgard. Omnipotent Odin hid and plotted to defeat the celestial host.

A demon named Haftan had a son whom Odin stole in infancy and raised. Because demons and celestials were enemies, Odin nurtured the child to provoke war and used magic to turn him into a warrior. This Hadding marched toward Asgard for revenge, refusing all other pursuits and leaving his hair uncut until his mission ended.

On the way Loki captured him, tied him to a tree, and posted guards. Hadding sang a magic song that put them to sleep; another killed a ravenous wolf; a third shattered his bonds. Odin had taught him these potent songs, though such magic had not saved Odin himself.

Hadding then met a graceful woman who courted him. He refused while engaged in vengeance, but she pleaded for love. She was the giantess Hardgrep, able to assume any form, from a cosmic giant to a supremely beautiful maiden. When Hadding remained unmoved and cried only for war, she took male form, armed herself, and accompanied him.

After many dangers, a demon’s curse caused her death by a “great hand,” a hand capable of subduing and destroying the cosmos, appearing and vanishing at will. Whose hand was it? The mythologist does not say. How did it possess power unavailable even to God? There is no...

---NOTES---
Part 7 begins as a continuation of the Teutonic creation discussion in `maji_kadavulgal6.md`. It ends after `விளக்கம்`, completed by `இடையாது` at the start of `maji_kadavulgal8.md`; the English ellipsis marks this boundary.

The printed/OCR date is damaged as `21-]/2-49`; it is represented conservatively as `21-?/2-49` only in the translation and remains verbatim in the Tamil source.

Names follow conventional English forms where identifiable: `அதும்லா` (Audhumla), `யெமர்` (Ymir), `ஓடின்` (Odin), `ஆஸ்கார்ட்` (Asgard), `ஜோர்ட்` (Jord), `தார்` (Thor), `பால்டர்` (Balder), `பிரிக்` (Frigg), `ஹெய்மிடால்` (Heimdall), `லாக்` (Loki), `ஸ்லீட்னர்` (Sleipnir), `நிஜார்ட்` (Njord), `உல்` (Ull), `ஹாப்டன்` (Haftan), `ஹாடிங்` (Hadding), and `ஹார்ட் கிரெப்` (Hardgrep). Tamil spellings are retained unchanged in the source block.

Several historical/mythic relationships in the source are compressed or nonstandard. The translation follows the source’s argument rather than silently reconciling it with modern Norse scholarship.
